\title{De Morgan laws for quantifiers}
\date{2026-02-05T14:57:15Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{The quantifiers #{\forall} and #{\exists} have de Morgan-like equivalences: ##{\begin{align*}
  \neg(\forall x:D\st P(x)) &\equiv \exists x:D\st \neg P(x) \\
  \neg(\exists x:D\st P(x)) &\equiv \forall x:D\st \neg P(x)
\end{align*}}
Intuitively, if #{P(x)} is not true for \em{all} #{x}, then it must be false for at least one; likewise, if it is not the case that there is some #{x} for which #{P(x)} is true, then #{P(x)} is false for all #{x}.
}
\p{Alternatively, if we remember that #{\forall} is like a big AND and #{\exists} is like a big OR, then we can see these as actual instances of the de Morgan laws for #{\land} and #{\lor}.}

\transclude{004Z}

